miadi-feedback: an issue opened on a Miadi repository wakes a seat
====================================================================

For a maintainer's host. A tester installs nothing of this: they open an issue
with one of the kit's forms (install, music, question), and `miadi-config report
--url <form>` fills in their host's facts.

Turn it on, once per user, from the shell where agents start (so gh, mct, claude
and node are on PATH):

    miadi-feedback enable      # writes ~/.config/miadi/feedback.env, enables the timer
    miadi-feedback check
    miadi-feedback poll --dry-run
    miadi-feedback status

A channel is /etc/miadi/feedback.d/<channel>.conf: the repository, the label its
form applies, SINCE and EXPIRES, the team and folder of its seats, the brief they
follow and the models they may run on. A file of the same name under
~/.config/miadi/feedback.d/ is read after it. A new kind of feedback is one more
file and one more brief in /usr/share/miadi-feedback/briefs/.

Each new issue gets one seat, a tmux session `feedback-<channel>-<repo>-<n>-<stamp>`
started with `mct new`, so tide and tmux restore bring it back after a reboot.
Its model is the channel model nearest to the worker Episode 564's chooser (Jev)
picks. An author who is the repository's owner, a member, a collaborator or listed
by `miadi-feedback trust <login>` gets a seat that may ship a minor fix; any other
author gets a seat in plan mode, and the maintainer approves its plan.

A seat ends with `miadi-feedback decide <owner/repo#n> <decision> "<what>"`, one of
shipped, answered, no-change, waiting, needs-guillaume: the issue's label and a
line in ~/.local/state/miadi-feedback/decisions.jsonl. An answer from the author on
a `waiting` issue wakes a new seat. At most 4 seats hold undecided issues at once
(MIADI_FEEDBACK_MAX_SEATS). After EXPIRES a channel posts what it handled to its
TRACKING issue, once, and reads nothing more.

Ref: jgwill/miadi-orchestration-kit#109
